8. COLOUR ENGINE (write in-house, pure TypeScript, no dependencies)  [MUST]
====================================================================================================

File suggestion: /lib/color/*.ts   (contrast.ts, oklch.ts, gamut.ts, analyze.ts, recipes.ts, prng.ts). Fully unit-tested (section 16).

8.1 Contrast
  lin(c) = c <= 0.04045 ? c/12.92 : ((c+0.055)/1.055)^2.4          (c in 0..1)
  Y(hex) = 0.2126*lin(R) + 0.7152*lin(G) + 0.0722*lin(B)
  contrast(a,b) = (max(Ya,Yb)+0.05) / (min(Ya,Yb)+0.05)
  Colours with alpha: composite over the background sample first (out = a*fg + (1-a)*bg), then compute contrast.
  Test vectors: contrast(#000000,#FFFFFF)=21.00 | contrast(#777777,#FFFFFF)=4.48 | contrast(#767676,#FFFFFF)=4.54 | contrast(#121212,#FAFAF9)=17.9 (+-0.1)

8.2 OKLab / OKLCH (Bjorn Ottosson) - use for all hue / chroma / lightness manipulation
  sRGB -> linear -> LMS:  l=0.4122214708R+0.5363325363G+0.0514459929B ; m=0.2119034982R+0.6806995451G+0.1073969566B ; s=0.0883024619R+0.2817188376G+0.6299787005B
  l_=cbrt(l) m_=cbrt(m) s_=cbrt(s)
  L=0.2104542553l_+0.7936177850m_-0.0040720468s_ ; a=1.9779984951l_-2.4285922050m_+0.4505937099s_ ; b=0.0259040371l_+0.7827717662m_-0.8086757660s_
  C=sqrt(a^2+b^2) ; h=atan2(b,a) in degrees (0..360)
  inverse: l_=L+0.3963377774a+0.2158037573b ; m_=L-0.1055613458a-0.0638541728b ; s_=L-0.0894841775a-1.2914855480b ; cube them ->
  R=4.0767416621l-3.3077115913m+0.2309699292s ; G=-1.2684380046l+2.6097574011m-0.3413193965s ; B=-0.0041960863l-0.7034186147m+1.7076147010s -> gamma encode
  Gamut: if any channel is outside 0..1, binary-search the chroma downward (12 iterations) keeping L and h. Never clip channels blindly.
  Distance between colours: deltaE = sqrt((dL)^2 + (da)^2 + (db)^2) in OKLab (values around 0.02 = barely different, 0.1 = clearly different).

8.3 Background analysis  analyzeBackground(bg) -> BgProfile  [MUST]
  Render the resolved background (preset, solid, gradient or image) to an offscreen canvas 96x96 (OffscreenCanvas in a Web Worker when available;
  for presets/gradients you may rasterise the CSS layers analytically). Then take a 48x48 grid of samples, convert each to OKLab, and compute:
    meanL, meanC, meanHue (from the mean of a and b), p05L / p95L (5th and 95th percentile of L), complexity = clamp(stdDevL / 0.18, 0, 1),
    darkest = sample at p05L, lightest = sample at p95L,   tone = light (meanL>=0.72) | mid (0.45..0.72) | dark (<0.45),
    chromatic = meanC >= 0.04,   warm = hue in 15..75 or 330..360,  cool = hue in 180..270.
  Cache the profile per background signature (hash of the config). For catalogue presets, precompute offline and store in the catalogue.
  IMPORTANT worst-case rule: text must pass against BOTH the darkest and the lightest sample of the background, not only the average.
  For image backgrounds: compute server-side after upload (sharp or equivalent), store the 8x8 luminance grid and dominant colours with the asset.
  If the worst-case contrast for the page text is below 4.5, add a smart scrim: rgba(black or white, alpha) over the image, alpha = minimal value
  (step 0.05, max 0.55) that makes the contrast pass; tell the user once ("We added a soft overlay so text stays readable").

8.4 Hard rules every suggested set must satisfy (STRICT)  [MUST]
  T1  button text vs button fill (blended over the background if translucent) >= 4.5:1  (prefer >= 7:1 for the first/baseline suggestion)
  T2  if fill is transparent: button text vs worst-case background >= 4.5:1
  T3  affordance: max( fill vs worst-case background , border vs worst-case background ) >= 3:1  (WCAG 1.4.11)
  T4  focus ring colour vs background >= 3:1 (derive it; do not let the user lose it)
  T5  page text (name, bio) vs worst-case background >= 4.5:1; muted/bio text >= 4.5:1
  SOFT-tier exception: recipes marked SOFT (tonal / soft-tint) may fail T3 but must pass T1, T2, T5, and must show a small "Subtle" tag. Never
  offer SOFT recipes as the first card.

8.5 Tone-to-strategy table (what suits which background)  [MUST - implement as data, not if/else spaghetti]
  BG meanL        Recommended fills                           Text on fill        Border                    Avoid
  >= 0.92 (white) near-black tinted (L 0.15-0.22); deep accent   near-white           same as fill / 1.5px ink  pale fills, light greys
                  (L 0.42-0.55, C 0.12-0.20); outline ink
  0.78-0.92       same as above + tonal soft (fill L -0.07)     near-white / ink    fill L -0.06              fills within 0.05 L of bg
  0.62-0.78       dark ink (L 0.18-0.30), deep accent L<=0.50   white               same as fill              mid-tone fills (low contrast)
  0.45-0.62 (mid) pure extremes: ink L<=0.20 or paper L>=0.96   opposite extreme     2px opposite extreme      mid fills, same-hue mids
                  glass works well (blur 16px)
  0.28-0.45       light paper (L>=0.92), bright accent L 0.75-0.85  ink L<=0.20     light, 1.5px             dark fills close to bg
  < 0.28 (dark)   light paper L 0.94-0.98; neon outline (L 0.82-0.9,   ink          neon / light, glow        black fills, grey outlines
                  C 0.15-0.22); gold foil if warm; glass
  Accent hue relative to bg hue (chromatic bg): analogous = +-25..35 deg, complement = +180 (use +-160..200 jitter), triad = +-120,
  split-complement = +150 / +210. For neutral bg (meanC < 0.04) use the vibe accent or a seeded hue from the wheel.
  Chroma caps for fills: L<0.4 -> C<=0.14 ; 0.4-0.7 -> C<=0.20 ; >0.7 -> C<=0.16 (then gamut-reduce).

8.6 Auto text colour  bestText(fill, bgWorst)  [MUST]  returns whichever of ink (#0A0A0B tinted to the fill hue, C<=0.02) or paper (#FAFAF9 tinted)
  has the higher contrast with the fill; if neither reaches 4.5, move the FILL lightness in 0.02 steps (max 20 steps) toward whichever side helps.
8.7 Seeded randomness  [MUST]  mulberry32(seed) PRNG; seed = hash32(pageId + bgSignature + vibeId + refreshCount). Same inputs = same ideas (stable
  across reloads); refreshCount increments on each tap of the refresh icon, so ideas change on demand but remain reproducible for tests.

